#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Compare generated C exactly, or with explicitly mapped generated symbols.

Raw C cannot prove whether a generated-looking token is compiler-owned or
source-authored. Without sidecars this tool hashes/compares raw C exactly.
With one hash-bound sidecar per C file, only listed identifier/comment spans
are normalized. Repeated uses share a logical key; collisions, splits, missing
uses, literal changes and structural differences remain visible.

Usage:

    benchmarks/normalize_generated_c_symbols a.c            # print hash
    benchmarks/normalize_generated_c_symbols a.c b.c        # compare, exit 1 if different
    benchmarks/normalize_generated_c_symbols --emit a.c     # print normalized text
    benchmarks/normalize_generated_c_symbols --sidecar a.json a.c
    benchmarks/normalize_generated_c_symbols --sidecar a.json --sidecar b.json a.c b.c

Sidecar v1 is JSON with `version: 1`, `c_sha256` for exact UTF-8 C bytes, and
`locals`, a list of `{owner_definition_id, local_ordinal, scope_span,
emitted_identifier, declaration_span, reference_spans}` records, plus an
optional `declared_type` field (see below). It may also
contain `generated`, a list of `{kind, identity, spelling, spans}` records,
where kind is callable, static_closure, definition, variable_suffix,
definition_comment, type, temp, local_argument, local_source_binder, global,
or field. Spans are zero-based `[start, end]` character offsets into
the C text. Local spans cover full identifier tokens; generated spans cover
the exact spelling supplied. Every occurrence of a mapped spelling must be
listed. V1 local rows cover simple block-local declarations with primitive/
specifier sequences or explicit `struct|union|enum Tag` types.
Optional qualifiers and pointer stars may precede the local identifier, which
must be followed by `=`, `;`, `,`, or `[`. The scope is the innermost balanced
brace pair containing its declaration, and that brace pair must be a recognized
compound opening: a primitive/tagged-return function body, control body ending
`)`, naked nested block, `else`/`do`/label body, or assignment/return
statement expression ending `= (` / `return (`.
Unknown openings, type definitions, and initializer braces reject. Function
pointers and other complex declarators fail closed and remain raw-different.
A local row may instead carry a `declared_type` string naming a typedef the
declarator uses -- a `brp_ty...` projected type or a `blorp_...` runtime
typedef (`blorp_StackOption_*`, `blorp_List*`, and similarly spelled types) --
so the declaration/parameter can be recognized without the tool parsing C
declarators itself; the recorded type still must appear as the exact prefix
token, and function pointers/complex declarators remain rejected regardless.
The same declarator grammar, plus `declared_type`, also recognizes function
parameters: a local row whose declaration span falls inside a recognized
function's parameter list is accepted when the parameter's type prefix is a
primitive/tagged sequence or matches `declared_type`, followed directly by
`,` or `)`; its reference spans still must lie inside the function body scope.
C5c must emit only supported rows, or deliberately version and extend the
oracle or emit an independently validated compiler-owned binding marker
before relying on unsupported forms.
Every mapped identifier token is listed exactly once; a reference belongs to
the innermost containing declaration that precedes it. Logical and scoped
local keys occur in exactly one row. Directive tokens, member names, labels,
goto targets, tag names, and typedef names are excluded from local spans.
Directive detection follows C line splicing and treats leading block comments
as whitespace. V1 sidecars reject C whose post-splice text contains `%:` or
`??=` anywhere, since
those alternate preprocessor-marker spellings need preprocessing before local
identifier roles can be trusted. Raw comparison remains available.
Numeric IDs may drift: normalization numbers locals by structural scope and
declaration order, and other generated symbols by their first occurrence
within each family. Linked callable/closure and definition/comment rows must
agree on the raw identity payload within each file. The `field` kind covers
projected struct/union member spellings (`f0`, `f1`, ... and the existing
union payload slots `field0`, `field1`, ...) under one family, so an ordinal
rename of record members normalizes against a parent that still spells them
out. Members that keep fixed spellings across the projection -- `header`,
`tag`, `release_mask`, `data`, the `Ok`/`Err`/`Some` payload members, and the
runtime stats struct fields -- are simply never given `field` rows, since
their names do not match the family's grammar.

`--project-locals` is a second, sidecar-free mode for the transition period:
with no `--sidecar` given, it normalizes any identifier matching the local
(`brp_v<owner>_a<key>`, `brp_v<owner>_s<key>`, or the owner-less binder-id forms
`brp_v_<id>` and `brp_vn_<id>`, combined into one family since this mode cannot
distinguish them), temp (`__t...`), type (`brp_ty...`),
global (`brp_g...`), or field (`f<N>`/`field<N>`) grammars, by first occurrence
per family. Local/temp/type/global ordinals reset at each top-level
brace-enclosed body (approximating per-function scope); field ordinals are
file-wide, since record members are referenced across many function bodies.
Union/enum variant symbols are also normalized file-wide, as substrings of an
identifier (they appear inside `__instance_<ctor>`, `__init_<ctor>` and
`__blorp_reuse_<Type>_<ctor>`): a constructor is `brp_c_<id>` or the older
`__def_<id>_<Variant>`, a tag is `brp_t_<id>` or the older `TAG_<Type>_<Variant>`.
This is weaker than a sidecar: it has no owner/scope information, so it
cannot tell two different bindings that happen to alternate spellings apart,
and it matches identifiers by spelling alone regardless of C role (a field
access, a declarator, and an unrelated local sharing a matched spelling are
all rewritten the same way). It also cannot know which records are
foreign-ABI-constrained and must keep their spellings; without a sidecar,
foreign-ABI record fields are compared raw like any other unmapped spelling.
Use `--project-locals` only to check a projection landing against its parent
before the compiler emits sidecars.

Exit status is 0 when the hashes agree, 1 when they do not, and 2 on a usage or
I/O error.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import bisect
import hashlib
import json
import re
import sys
from dataclasses import dataclass
from pathlib import Path

TOKEN_RE = re.compile(
    r"(?P<comment>/\*[\s\S]*?\*/|//[^\n]*)"
    r"|(?P<literal>\"(?:\\.|[^\"\\])*\"|'(?:\\.|[^'\\])*')"
    r"|(?P<identifier>[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z_0-9]*)"
    r"|(?P<number>[0-9]+)"
    r"|(?P<brace>[{}])"
    r"|(?P<punct>[#;=,*()\[\]+\-/&|<>?:.])"
)
# A local is spelled either from its owner and ordinal (`brp_v<owner>_a<key>` /
# `brp_v<owner>_s<key>`) or, when the compiler spells it from binder identity,
# `brp_v_<base62 id>` for an authored binder and `brp_vn_<base62 |id|>` for one
# a pass minted. The id forms have no owner segment: `brp_v` followed directly
# by `_` (or `n_`) can never be an owner form, whose owner is non-empty, except
# where `brp_vn_<a|s><key>` happens to read as owner `n`; both mean "a local".
LOCAL_ID_PATTERN = r"brp_vn?_[A-Za-z0-9]+"
LOCAL_ARGUMENT_PATTERN = r"brp_v[A-Za-z0-9]+_a[A-Za-z0-9]+|" + LOCAL_ID_PATTERN
LOCAL_SOURCE_BINDER_PATTERN = r"brp_v[A-Za-z0-9]+_s[A-Za-z0-9]+|" + LOCAL_ID_PATTERN

GENERATED_GRAMMARS = {
    "callable": re.compile(r"brp_[A-Za-z0-9]+"),
    "static_closure": re.compile(r"__sc_brp_[A-Za-z0-9]+"),
    "definition": re.compile(r"__def_[0-9]+_"),
    "variable_suffix": re.compile(r"[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z_0-9]*__v[0-9]+"),
    "definition_comment": re.compile(r"def_id=[0-9]+"),
    "type": re.compile(r"brp_ty[A-Za-z0-9]+"),
    "temp": re.compile(r"__t[A-Za-z0-9]+"),
    "local_argument": re.compile(LOCAL_ARGUMENT_PATTERN),
    "local_source_binder": re.compile(LOCAL_SOURCE_BINDER_PATTERN),
    "global": re.compile(r"brp_g[A-Za-z0-9]+"),
    "field": re.compile(r"f[0-9]+|field[0-9]+"),
}

# Whole-identifier "generated" kinds: the entire identifier token is the
# mapped spelling, so a listed row must be checked against every occurrence
# of that exact token in the file (see the "unlisted generated occurrence"
# scan below), and its span must exactly cover a token boundary.
WHOLE_IDENTIFIER_GENERATED_KINDS = (
    "callable", "static_closure", "variable_suffix",
    "type", "temp", "local_argument", "local_source_binder", "global", "field",
)

# Type names a `declared_type` field on a local row may name: projected
# `brp_ty...` types, and `blorp_...`-spelled runtime typedefs such as
# `blorp_StackOption_Int` or `blorp_List`. The recorded name is only the base
# type identifier; pointer stars remain separate declarator tokens.
TYPEDEF_NAME_RE = re.compile(r"brp_ty[A-Za-z0-9]+|blorp_[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z_0-9]*")

REQUIRED_LOCAL_FIELDS = frozenset({
    "owner_definition_id", "local_ordinal", "scope_span",
    "emitted_identifier", "declaration_span", "reference_spans",
})
OPTIONAL_LOCAL_FIELDS = frozenset({"declared_type"})


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Lexeme:
    match: re.Match[str]
    source_start: int
    source_end: int
    logical_start: int
    logical_end: int

    def group(self, name: str | int) -> str | None:
        return self.match.group(name)

    def start(self) -> int:
        return self.source_start

    def end(self) -> int:
        return self.source_end

    def span(self) -> tuple[int, int]:
        return self.source_start, self.source_end


def c_lexemes(text: str) -> tuple[list[Lexeme], str]:
    """Apply C line splicing before lexing, retaining original offsets."""
    chunks: list[str] = []
    event_positions: list[int] = []
    cumulative_removed: list[int] = []
    previous = 0
    removed = 0
    for splice in re.finditer(r"\\\r?\n", text):
        chunks.append(text[previous:splice.start()])
        event_positions.append(splice.start() - removed)
        removed += splice.end() - splice.start()
        cumulative_removed.append(removed)
        previous = splice.end()
    chunks.append(text[previous:])
    logical = "".join(chunks)

    def start_offset(position: int) -> int:
        index = bisect.bisect_right(event_positions, position) - 1
        return position + (cumulative_removed[index] if index >= 0 else 0)

    def end_offset(position: int) -> int:
        index = bisect.bisect_left(event_positions, position) - 1
        return position + (cumulative_removed[index] if index >= 0 else 0)

    return ([Lexeme(match, start_offset(match.start()), end_offset(match.end()),
                    match.start(), match.end())
             for match in TOKEN_RE.finditer(logical)], logical)


def directive_spans(lexemes: list[Lexeme], logical: str) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:
    """Find logical-line directives after whitespace and block comments."""
    spans: list[tuple[int, int]] = []
    directive_start: int | None = None
    directive_end = 0
    leading_only = True
    previous_end = 0
    for lexeme in lexemes:
        gap = logical[previous_end:lexeme.logical_start]
        if "\n" in gap:
            if directive_start is not None:
                spans.append((directive_start, directive_end))
                directive_start = None
            leading_only = True
            gap = gap.rsplit("\n", 1)[-1]
        if gap.strip():
            leading_only = False
        if directive_start is not None:
            directive_end = lexeme.end()
        elif lexeme.group("comment") is not None:
            pass  # Phase-3 block comments act as whitespace, even across lines.
        elif lexeme.group(0) == "#" and leading_only:
            directive_start = lexeme.start()
            directive_end = lexeme.end()
        else:
            leading_only = False
        previous_end = lexeme.logical_end
    if directive_start is not None:
        spans.append((directive_start, directive_end))
    return spans


def apply_sidecar(text: str, sidecar: dict) -> str:
    """Replace only exact, declared local-token spans from a versioned map."""
    if not isinstance(sidecar, dict) or set(sidecar) - {"version", "c_sha256", "locals", "generated"}:
        raise ValueError("sidecar must be an object with known fields")
    if type(sidecar.get("version")) is not int or sidecar["version"] != 1:
        raise ValueError("sidecar version must be 1")
    digest = sidecar.get("c_sha256")
    if not isinstance(digest, str) or not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9a-f]{64}", digest):
        raise ValueError("sidecar c_sha256 must be a lowercase SHA-256 hex digest")
    if digest != hashlib.sha256(text.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest():
        raise ValueError("sidecar C hash does not match input")
    lexemes, logical = c_lexemes(text)
    if "%:" in logical or "??=" in logical:
        raise ValueError("sidecar v1 does not support alternate preprocessor markers %: or ??=")
    rows = sidecar.get("locals")
    if not isinstance(rows, list):
        raise ValueError("sidecar locals must be a list")
    starts = [match.start() for match in lexemes]
    tokens = {match.span(): text[match.start():match.end()] for match in lexemes
              if match.group("identifier") is not None}
    directives = directive_spans(lexemes, logical)
    directive_starts = [start for start, _ in directives]

    def in_directive(position: int) -> bool:
        index = bisect.bisect_right(directive_starts, position) - 1
        return index >= 0 and position < directives[index][1]
    qualifiers = {"const", "volatile", "static", "register", "restrict", "extern", "_Atomic"}
    primitives = {"void", "char", "short", "int", "long", "float", "double",
                  "signed", "unsigned", "_Bool", "_Complex", "_Imaginary"}

    def valid_type_prefix(prefix: list[str], expected_typedef: str | None = None) -> bool:
        if not prefix or any(token != "*" and not re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z_0-9]*", token)
                             for token in prefix):
            return False
        words = [token for token in prefix if token != "*" and token not in qualifiers]
        if not words:
            return False
        if expected_typedef is not None:
            return words == [expected_typedef]
        if all(word in primitives for word in words):
            return True
        if len(words) == 2 and words[0] in ("struct", "union", "enum"):
            return True
        return False

    brace_stack: list[int] = []
    scope_pairs: dict[tuple[int, int], int] = {}
    compound_openings: set[int] = set()
    # Char-offset parameter-list span (start of "(", end of matching ")") for
    # each recognized function body, keyed by that body's opening "{" offset.
    # Lets a `locals` row express a parameter -- a declarator that lies before
    # the function's own brace scope -- without re-parsing the signature.
    function_body_opens: dict[int, tuple[int, int]] = {}
    for index, lexeme in enumerate(lexemes):
        if in_directive(lexeme.start()):
            continue
        if lexeme.group("brace") == "{":
            previous = index - 1
            while previous >= 0 and lexemes[previous].group(0) not in (";", "{", "}"):
                previous -= 1
            prefix = [token.group(0) for token in lexemes[previous + 1:index]
                      if token.group("comment") is None and not in_directive(token.start())]
            is_naked_block = bool(brace_stack) and not prefix
            is_else_or_do = prefix in (["else"], ["do"])
            is_label = (len(prefix) == 2 and prefix[1] == ":"
                        and re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z_0-9]*", prefix[0]))
            is_case = (len(prefix) >= 3 and prefix[0] == "case" and prefix[-1] == ":")
            is_control = (prefix[-1:] == [")"] and prefix[:1] in
                          (["if"], ["for"], ["while"], ["switch"])
                          or prefix[:2] == ["else", "if"] and prefix[-1:] == [")"])
            is_statement_expression = bool(brace_stack) and prefix[-2:] in (["=", "("], ["return", "("])
            is_function = False
            function_param_range: tuple[int, int] | None = None
            if not brace_stack and prefix[-1:] == [")"] and "(" in prefix:
                opening_paren = prefix.index("(")
                is_function = (opening_paren >= 2
                               and re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z_0-9]*", prefix[opening_paren - 1])
                               and valid_type_prefix(prefix[:opening_paren - 1]))
                if is_function:
                    prefix_lexemes = [token for token in lexemes[previous + 1:index]
                                      if token.group("comment") is None
                                      and not in_directive(token.start())]
                    function_param_range = (prefix_lexemes[opening_paren].start(),
                                            prefix_lexemes[-1].end())
            if (is_naked_block or is_else_or_do or is_label or is_case or is_control
                    or is_statement_expression or is_function):
                compound_openings.add(lexeme.start())
            if is_function and function_param_range is not None:
                function_body_opens[lexeme.start()] = function_param_range
            brace_stack.append(lexeme.start())
        elif lexeme.group("brace") == "}":
            if not brace_stack:
                raise ValueError("unbalanced C braces")
            opening = brace_stack.pop()
            scope_pairs[(opening, lexeme.end())] = 0
    if brace_stack:
        raise ValueError("unbalanced C braces")
    scope_pairs = {span: index for index, span in enumerate(sorted(scope_pairs))}

    def containing_lexeme(start: int, end: int) -> re.Match[str] | None:
        index = bisect.bisect_right(starts, start) - 1
        if index >= 0 and end <= lexemes[index].end():
            return lexemes[index]
        return None
    keys: set[tuple[int, int]] = set()
    scoped_names: set[tuple[tuple[int, int], str]] = set()
    mapped_names: set[str] = set()
    replacements: dict[tuple[int, int], str] = {}
    local_rows: list[tuple[tuple[int, int], tuple[int, int], str, list[tuple[int, int]]]] = []
    token_indices = {lexeme.span(): index for index, lexeme in enumerate(lexemes)
                     if lexeme.group("identifier") is not None}

    def adjacent_index(index: int, direction: int) -> int | None:
        index += direction
        while 0 <= index < len(lexemes) and lexemes[index].group("comment") is not None:
            index += direction
        return index if 0 <= index < len(lexemes) else None

    def require_value_identifier_role(span: tuple[int, int]) -> None:
        """Exclude C namespaces and syntax sites that cannot name a local value."""
        if in_directive(span[0]):
            raise ValueError("local span is inside a preprocessor directive")
        index = token_indices.get(span)
        if index is None:
            return
        previous_index = adjacent_index(index, -1)
        before_previous_index = adjacent_index(previous_index, -1) if previous_index is not None else None
        following_index = adjacent_index(index, 1)
        previous = lexemes[previous_index].group(0) if previous_index is not None else None
        before_previous = (lexemes[before_previous_index].group(0)
                           if before_previous_index is not None else None)
        following = lexemes[following_index].group(0) if following_index is not None else None
        if (previous in (".", "goto", "struct", "union", "enum")
                or previous == ">" and before_previous == "-"
                or following == ":"):
            raise ValueError("local span is in a non-value C identifier role")
        statement_start = index - 1
        while statement_start >= 0 and lexemes[statement_start].group(0) not in (";", "{", "}"):
            statement_start -= 1
        if any(lexemes[position].group(0) == "typedef"
               for position in range(statement_start + 1, index)):
            raise ValueError("local span is in a typedef declaration")
        containing_scopes = [candidate for candidate in scope_pairs
                             if candidate[0] < span[0] and span[1] < candidate[1]]
        if containing_scopes:
            innermost = min(containing_scopes, key=lambda candidate: candidate[1] - candidate[0])
            if innermost[0] not in compound_openings:
                raise ValueError("local span is inside a non-compound C scope")

    def is_simple_declaration(span: tuple[int, int], declared_type: str | None = None) -> bool:
        """Recognize only generated C's simple local type/name declarators."""
        index = token_indices.get(span)
        if index is None:
            return False
        previous = index - 1
        while previous >= 0 and lexemes[previous].group(0) not in (";", "{", "}"):
            previous -= 1
        prefix = [token.group(0) for token in lexemes[previous + 1:index]
                  if token.group("comment") is None]
        next_index = index + 1
        while next_index < len(lexemes) and lexemes[next_index].group("comment") is not None:
            next_index += 1
        next_token = lexemes[next_index].group(0) if next_index < len(lexemes) else None
        if next_token not in ("=", ";", ",", "["):
            return False
        return valid_type_prefix(prefix, declared_type)

    def is_simple_parameter_declaration(span: tuple[int, int], param_range: tuple[int, int],
                                        declared_type: str | None = None) -> bool:
        """Recognize a simple parameter declarator inside a function's own parameter list."""
        index = token_indices.get(span)
        if index is None:
            return False
        start, end = param_range
        if not (start <= span[0] and span[1] <= end):
            return False
        previous = index - 1
        while previous >= 0 and lexemes[previous].group(0) not in (",", "("):
            previous -= 1
        prefix = [token.group(0) for token in lexemes[previous + 1:index]
                  if token.group("comment") is None]
        next_index = index + 1
        while next_index < len(lexemes) and lexemes[next_index].group("comment") is not None:
            next_index += 1
        next_token = lexemes[next_index].group(0) if next_index < len(lexemes) else None
        if next_token not in (",", ")"):
            return False
        return valid_type_prefix(prefix, declared_type)

    def checked_span(value: object, label: str) -> tuple[int, int]:
        if (not isinstance(value, list) or len(value) != 2
                or any(type(offset) is not int for offset in value)):
            raise ValueError(f"malformed {label} span")
        start, end = value
        if start < 0 or end > len(text) or start >= end:
            raise ValueError(f"out-of-bounds {label} span")
        return start, end

    for row in rows:
        if (not isinstance(row, dict)
                or not REQUIRED_LOCAL_FIELDS <= set(row) <= REQUIRED_LOCAL_FIELDS | OPTIONAL_LOCAL_FIELDS):
            raise ValueError("local row must be an object")
        owner = row.get("owner_definition_id")
        local = row.get("local_ordinal")
        scope = checked_span(row.get("scope_span"), "scope")
        symbol = row.get("emitted_identifier")
        declaration = row.get("declaration_span")
        references = row.get("reference_spans")
        declared_type = row.get("declared_type") if "declared_type" in row else None
        if declared_type is not None and (not isinstance(declared_type, str)
                                          or not TYPEDEF_NAME_RE.fullmatch(declared_type)):
            raise ValueError("local declared_type must be a recognized typedef name")
        if (type(owner) is not int or owner < 0 or type(local) is not int or local < 0
                or not isinstance(symbol, str) or not re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z_0-9]*", symbol)
                or not isinstance(references, list)):
            raise ValueError("malformed local row")
        if scope not in scope_pairs:
            raise ValueError("scope span must be a balanced C brace pair")
        if scope[0] not in compound_openings:
            raise ValueError("local scope is not a compound statement")
        key = (owner, local)
        scoped_name = (scope, symbol)
        if key in keys or scoped_name in scoped_names:
            raise ValueError("split or merged local mapping")
        keys.add(key)
        scoped_names.add(scoped_name)
        mapped_names.add(symbol)
        all_spans = [checked_span(span, "local") for span in [declaration, *references]]
        for span in all_spans:
            require_value_identifier_role(span)
        declaration_span = all_spans[0]
        reference_spans = all_spans[1:]
        param_range = function_body_opens.get(scope[0])
        is_parameter = (param_range is not None
                        and param_range[0] <= declaration_span[0]
                        and declaration_span[1] <= param_range[1])
        if is_parameter:
            if (tokens.get(declaration_span) != symbol
                    or not is_simple_parameter_declaration(declaration_span, param_range, declared_type)):
                raise ValueError("local declaration is not a recognized C declarator")
        else:
            containing_scopes = [candidate for candidate in scope_pairs
                                 if candidate[0] < declaration_span[0]
                                 and declaration_span[1] < candidate[1]]
            if not containing_scopes or scope != min(containing_scopes,
                                                      key=lambda candidate: candidate[1] - candidate[0]):
                raise ValueError("local declaration must use its innermost brace scope")
            if not (scope[0] < declaration_span[0] and declaration_span[1] < scope[1]):
                raise ValueError("local span lies outside scope")
            if (tokens.get(declaration_span) != symbol
                    or not is_simple_declaration(declaration_span, declared_type)):
                raise ValueError("local declaration is not a recognized C declarator")
        if any(not (scope[0] < start and end < scope[1]) for start, end in reference_spans):
            raise ValueError("local span lies outside scope")
        if any(is_simple_declaration(span, declared_type) for span in reference_spans):
            raise ValueError("local reference cannot be a declaration")
        if any(start <= declaration_span[0] for start, _ in reference_spans):
            raise ValueError("local reference precedes its declaration")
        local_rows.append((scope, declaration_span, symbol, all_spans))
        for bounds in all_spans:
            if tokens.get(bounds) != symbol or bounds in replacements:
                raise ValueError("local span is not a unique exact identifier token")
            replacements[bounds] = ""
    declarations_by_scope: dict[tuple[int, int], list[int]] = {}
    for scope, declaration, _, _ in local_rows:
        declarations_by_scope.setdefault(scope, []).append(declaration[0])
    for scope, declaration, _, spans in local_rows:
        rank = sorted(declarations_by_scope[scope]).index(declaration[0])
        marker = f"@@local:{scope_pairs[scope]}:{rank}@@"
        for span in spans:
            replacements[span] = marker
    uncovered = [span for span, token in tokens.items()
                 if token in mapped_names and span not in replacements]
    if uncovered:
        raise ValueError(f"unlisted local identifier occurrence at {uncovered[0][0]}")
    for row_scope, declaration, symbol, spans in local_rows:
        for reference in spans[1:]:
            candidates = [(scope, declared_at) for scope, declared_at, name, _ in local_rows
                          if name == symbol and scope[0] < reference[0]
                          and reference[1] < scope[1] and declared_at[0] < reference[0]]
            if not candidates:
                raise ValueError("local reference has no preceding declaration")
            innermost_scope, owning_declaration = min(candidates,
                                                        key=lambda candidate: candidate[0][1] - candidate[0][0])
            if (innermost_scope, owning_declaration) != (row_scope, declaration):
                raise ValueError("local reference belongs to another scoped declaration")
    generated = sidecar.get("generated", [])
    if not isinstance(generated, list):
        raise ValueError("sidecar generated must be a list")
    seen_generated: set[tuple[str, str]] = set()
    generated_ids: set[tuple[str, str]] = set()
    linked_payloads: dict[tuple[str, str], str] = {}
    generated_rows: list[tuple[str, str, list[tuple[int, int]]]] = []
    for row in generated:
        if not isinstance(row, dict) or set(row) != {"kind", "identity", "spelling", "spans"}:
            raise ValueError("generated row must be an object")
        kind = row.get("kind")
        identity = row.get("identity")
        spelling = row.get("spelling")
        spans = row.get("spans")
        if (not isinstance(kind, str) or kind not in GENERATED_GRAMMARS
                or not isinstance(identity, str)
                or not re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z0-9_]+", identity)
                or not isinstance(spelling, str) or not isinstance(spans, list)
                or not spans or (kind, spelling) in seen_generated
                or (kind, identity) in generated_ids):
            raise ValueError("malformed or duplicate generated row")
        seen_generated.add((kind, spelling))
        generated_ids.add((kind, identity))
        if not GENERATED_GRAMMARS[kind].fullmatch(spelling):
            raise ValueError("generated spelling does not match kind")
        if kind in ("callable", "static_closure"):
            linked_key = ("callable", identity)
            payload = spelling.removeprefix("__sc_brp_").removeprefix("brp_")
        elif kind in ("definition", "definition_comment"):
            linked_key = ("definition", identity)
            payload = spelling.removeprefix("__def_").removesuffix("_").removeprefix("def_id=")
        else:
            linked_key = None
            payload = None
        if linked_key is not None:
            if linked_key in linked_payloads and linked_payloads[linked_key] != payload:
                raise ValueError("linked generated spellings disagree on raw identity")
            linked_payloads[linked_key] = payload
        validated_spans: list[tuple[int, int]] = []
        for span in spans:
            start, end = checked_span(span, "generated")
            if text[start:end] != spelling or (start, end) in replacements:
                raise ValueError("generated span does not match exact spelling")
            containing = containing_lexeme(start, end)
            if containing is None or (kind == "definition_comment") != (containing.group("comment") is not None):
                raise ValueError("generated span has wrong lexical context")
            if kind != "definition_comment" and containing.group("identifier") is None:
                raise ValueError("generated span must be inside an identifier")
            if kind in WHOLE_IDENTIFIER_GENERATED_KINDS and (start, end) not in tokens:
                raise ValueError("generated span must cover full identifier")
            replacements[(start, end)] = ""
            validated_spans.append((start, end))
        generated_rows.append((kind, identity, validated_spans))
    family = {"callable": "callable", "static_closure": "callable",
              "definition": "definition", "definition_comment": "definition",
              "variable_suffix": "variable_suffix", "type": "type", "temp": "temp",
              "local_argument": "local_argument", "local_source_binder": "local_source_binder",
              "global": "global", "field": "field"}
    first_by_family: dict[tuple[str, str], int] = {}
    for kind, identity, spans in generated_rows:
        key = (family[kind], identity)
        first_by_family[key] = min(first_by_family.get(key, len(text)), min(start for start, _ in spans))
    ordinals: dict[tuple[str, str], int] = {}
    for key in sorted(first_by_family, key=lambda item: (item[0], first_by_family[item])):
        ordinals[key] = sum(1 for other in ordinals if other[0] == key[0])
    for kind, identity, spans in generated_rows:
        marker = f"@@{kind}:{ordinals[(family[kind], identity)]}@@"
        for span in spans:
            replacements[span] = marker
    for lexeme in lexemes:
        if lexeme.group("identifier") is not None:
            whole = lexeme.group(0)
            candidates = [(kind, whole, lexeme.span()) for kind in WHOLE_IDENTIFIER_GENERATED_KINDS]
            candidates.extend(("definition", match.group(0),
                               (lexeme.start() + match.start(), lexeme.start() + match.end()))
                              for match in re.finditer(r"__def_[0-9]+_", whole))
        elif lexeme.group("comment") is not None:
            candidates = [("definition_comment", match.group(0),
                           (lexeme.start() + match.start(), lexeme.start() + match.end()))
                          for match in re.finditer(r"def_id=[0-9]+", lexeme.group(0))]
        else:
            continue
        for kind, spelling, span in candidates:
            if (kind, spelling) in seen_generated and span not in replacements:
                raise ValueError(f"unlisted generated occurrence at {span[0]}")
    result = []
    cursor = 0
    for (start, end), replacement in sorted(replacements.items()):
        if start < cursor:
            raise ValueError("overlapping local spans")
        result.extend((text[cursor:start], replacement))
        cursor = end
    result.append(text[cursor:])
    return "".join(result)


def normalize(text: str, sidecar: dict | None = None) -> str:
    """Return exact C unless a validated sidecar authorizes specific rewrites."""
    return apply_sidecar(text, sidecar) if sidecar is not None else text


# Grammars used by --project-locals. "local" folds the argument and source-
# binder families together, since this sidecar-free mode has no owner/scope
# information to tell them apart -- see the module docstring.
PROJECT_LOCALS_PER_BODY_GRAMMARS = {
    "local": re.compile(r"brp_v[A-Za-z0-9]+_[as][A-Za-z0-9]+|" + LOCAL_ID_PATTERN),
    "temp": GENERATED_GRAMMARS["temp"],
    "type": GENERATED_GRAMMARS["type"],
    "global": GENERATED_GRAMMARS["global"],
}
PROJECT_LOCALS_FILE_WIDE_GRAMMARS = {
    "field": GENERATED_GRAMMARS["field"],
}
# Union/enum variant symbols. A variant symbol is a program-wide name that also
# appears inside derived identifiers (`__instance_<ctor>`, `__init_<ctor>`,
# `__blorp_reuse_<Type>_<ctor>`), so these grammars are matched as substrings
# of an identifier, file-wide. Each family accepts both the id-derived spelling
# (`brp_c_<b62 def id>` constructor, `brp_t_<b62 def id>` tag) and the
# name-derived spelling it replaced (`__def_<id>_<Variant>`,
# `TAG_<Type>_<Variant>`), so a parent using the old spelling normalizes
# against a candidate using the new one. A variant with no definition identity
# keeps the old spelling in both trees and normalizes with itself.
PROJECT_LOCALS_VARIANT_SUBSTRING_GRAMMARS = {
    "variant_constructor": re.compile(r"__def_[0-9]+_[A-Za-z0-9_]*|brp_c_[A-Za-z0-9]+"),
    "variant_tag": re.compile(r"(?<![A-Za-z0-9_])(?:TAG_[A-Za-z0-9_]+|brp_t_[A-Za-z0-9]+)"),
}


def project_locals(text: str) -> str:
    """Heuristically canonicalize local/temp/type/global/field-looking identifiers.

    Used only when no sidecar is available, to check a projection landing
    against its parent before the compiler emits sidecars. Local, temp, type,
    and global occurrences are renumbered by first occurrence per family,
    reset at each top-level brace-enclosed body (approximating a function);
    field occurrences are renumbered file-wide, since record members are
    referenced across many function bodies. This is weaker than a sidecar: it
    has no owner/scope information, so a spelling match alone decides family
    membership regardless of C role (declarator, reference, or member
    access), and two distinct bindings that happen to alternate spellings
    normalize the same way. Foreign-ABI record fields, which must keep fixed
    spellings, are indistinguishable from any other field here and are
    compared raw like any other unmapped spelling.
    """
    lexemes, _ = c_lexemes(text)
    depth = 0
    body_id = -1
    next_body_id = 0
    occurrences: list[tuple[int, str, str, tuple[int, int]]] = []
    for lexeme in lexemes:
        if lexeme.group("brace") == "{":
            if depth == 0:
                body_id = next_body_id
                next_body_id += 1
            depth += 1
        elif lexeme.group("brace") == "}":
            depth -= 1
            if depth == 0:
                body_id = -1
        elif lexeme.group("identifier") is not None:
            whole = lexeme.group(0)
            for family, grammar in PROJECT_LOCALS_PER_BODY_GRAMMARS.items():
                if grammar.fullmatch(whole):
                    occurrences.append((body_id, family, whole, lexeme.span()))
                    break
            else:
                for family, grammar in PROJECT_LOCALS_FILE_WIDE_GRAMMARS.items():
                    if grammar.fullmatch(whole):
                        occurrences.append((-1, family, whole, lexeme.span()))
                        break
                else:
                    identifier_start = lexeme.start()
                    for family, grammar in PROJECT_LOCALS_VARIANT_SUBSTRING_GRAMMARS.items():
                        for found in grammar.finditer(whole):
                            occurrences.append((
                                -1,
                                family,
                                found.group(0),
                                (identifier_start + found.start(), identifier_start + found.end()),
                            ))
    first_seen: dict[tuple[int, str, str], int] = {}
    for scope, family, whole, span in occurrences:
        key = (scope, family, whole)
        first_seen[key] = min(first_seen.get(key, span[0]), span[0])
    grouped: dict[tuple[int, str], list[str]] = {}
    for scope, family, whole in first_seen:
        grouped.setdefault((scope, family), []).append(whole)
    ordinals: dict[tuple[int, str, str], int] = {}
    for (scope, family), names in grouped.items():
        for rank, whole in enumerate(sorted(names, key=lambda name: first_seen[(scope, family, name)])):
            ordinals[(scope, family, whole)] = rank
    replacements: dict[tuple[int, int], str] = {}
    for scope, family, whole, span in occurrences:
        replacements[span] = f"@@project:{family}:{scope}:{ordinals[(scope, family, whole)]}@@"
    result = []
    cursor = 0
    for (start, end), replacement in sorted(replacements.items()):
        result.extend((text[cursor:start], replacement))
        cursor = end
    result.append(text[cursor:])
    return "".join(result)


def read_c(path: Path) -> str:
    """Decode without newline translation so sidecar hashes exact file bytes."""
    return path.read_bytes().decode("utf-8")


def normalized_hash(path: Path, sidecar_path: Path | None = None) -> str:
    sidecar = json.loads(sidecar_path.read_text()) if sidecar_path else None
    return hashlib.sha256(normalize(read_c(path), sidecar).encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()


def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter
    )
    parser.add_argument("files", nargs="+", type=Path, help="one file to hash, or two to compare")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--emit", action="store_true", help="print the normalized text instead of a hash"
    )
    parser.add_argument("--sidecar", action="append", type=Path,
                        help="versioned local-identity JSON sidecar, once per C file")
    parser.add_argument("--project-locals", action="store_true",
                        help="transition-only heuristic: normalize local/temp/type/global/"
                             "field-looking identifiers by first occurrence when no sidecar "
                             "is available (weaker than a sidecar; see the module docstring)")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)

    if len(args.files) > 2:
        print("normalize_generated_c_symbols: pass one or two files", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if args.sidecar and len(args.sidecar) != len(args.files):
        print("normalize_generated_c_symbols: provide one sidecar per C file", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if args.project_locals and args.sidecar:
        print("normalize_generated_c_symbols: --project-locals cannot be combined with --sidecar",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    try:
        if args.emit:
            if len(args.files) != 1:
                print("normalize_generated_c_symbols: --emit takes one file", file=sys.stderr)
                return 2
            if args.project_locals:
                sys.stdout.write(project_locals(read_c(args.files[0])))
            else:
                sidecar = json.loads(args.sidecar[0].read_text()) if args.sidecar else None
                sys.stdout.write(normalize(read_c(args.files[0]), sidecar))
            return 0

        if args.project_locals:
            hashes = [(path, hashlib.sha256(project_locals(read_c(path)).encode("utf-8")).hexdigest())
                      for path in args.files]
        else:
            hashes = [(path, normalized_hash(path, args.sidecar[index] if args.sidecar else None))
                      for index, path in enumerate(args.files)]
    except (OSError, ValueError, UnicodeError, TypeError, AttributeError) as error:
        print(f"normalize_generated_c_symbols: {error}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    mode = ("normalized C with --project-locals" if args.project_locals else
            "normalized C with sidecar" if args.sidecar else "raw C")
    for path, digest in hashes:
        print(f"{mode} SHA-256: {digest}  {path}")

    if len(hashes) == 2:
        identical = hashes[0][1] == hashes[1][1]
        print(f"{mode}: IDENTICAL" if identical else f"{mode}: DIFFERENT")
        return 0 if identical else 1

    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
